\ Anaphora in two groups. it / other / them form one group; this / that form a
\ second. A group fixes the top two stack values at the first mention of any of
\ its words in the clause and holds them for the rest of it, so the two groups
\ can name different values on the same line. it and this push the first value,
\ other and that the second, them both in their original order. All five are
\ non-consuming and repeatable. A clause is one input line at top level and one
\ activation in compiled code: in a definition it / other / them are fixed at
\ entry, while this / that are fixed at their first mention in the body, which
\ must sit at the top level of the body.

\ === it / other / them fix at their first mention on the line ===
5 it * . cr            \ 25
clear
1 2 it .s cr           \ 1 2 2
clear
1 2 other .s cr        \ 1 2 1
clear
1 2 them .s cr         \ 1 2 1 2
clear

\ === the values hold for the rest of the line ===
1 2 it 3 4 it .s cr    \ 1 2 2 3 4 2 (the second mention reads the fixed value)
clear
3 4 them + it * . cr   \ 28 (them gives 3 4; it still names the 4)
clear
9 100 it + . cr        \ 200 (it names the 100, the top where it was mentioned)
clear

\ === one fixing serves all three words of the group ===
1 2 it other + . cr    \ 3 (it = 2, other = 1)
clear
1 2 them other + + . cr  \ 4 (them gives 1 2, other gives 1)
clear

\ === this / that fix as their own pair ===
1 2 this .s cr         \ 1 2 2
clear
1 2 that .s cr         \ 1 2 1
clear
1 2 this that - . cr   \ 1
clear

\ === the two groups fix independently ===
1 2 it 3 4 this that .s cr  \ 1 2 2 3 4 4 3
clear
1 2 this other + . cr  \ 4 (this pushes 2, so other is fixed over 1 2 2)
clear

\ === heap values alias, like dup ===
[ 1 2 3 ] it size . cr   \ 3
clear
[ 1 2 3 ] it 0 @i . cr   \ 1
clear

\ === a fixed value outlives the stack cell it came from ===
[ 4 5 ] this drop drop gc this size . cr   \ 2
clear

\ === a failed line restores the stack; the next line fixes afresh ===
8
1 0 %                  \ (division by zero; the line fails and the stack is restored)
it . cr                \ 8
clear

\ === in a file, each line fixes its own ===
": double it + ;
7 double . cr
5 it it * . cr
1 2 this that - . cr
" "/tmp/water_139_anaphora.h2o" write-file
"/tmp/water_139_anaphora.h2o" load    \ 14, then 25, then 1
clear

\ === definitions: it / other / them are the activation's stack ===
: square it it * ;
5 square . cr          \ 25
: late 2 * it + ;
10 late . cr           \ 30 (the body consumed the 10; it still names it)
: diff them - ;
3 4 diff . cr          \ -1
: under other ;
7 8 under . cr         \ 7
clear

\ === definitions: this / that fix where they are mentioned in the body ===
: doubled 2 * this + ;
7 doubled . cr         \ 28 (this names the doubled value where it names the 7)
: pair-diff this that - ;
3 4 pair-diff . cr     \ 1
clear

\ === it and this in one body: the entry value and the mention value ===
: both it 2 * this + ;
7 both .s cr           \ 7 28
clear

\ === it then them in one body (three compile passes) ===
: upgrade it them + + ;
3 4 upgrade . cr       \ 11
clear

\ === peek before receive: it and a receive see the same value ===
: mixed-receive | >x | it x + ;
6 mixed-receive . cr   \ 12
: all-receive |> x y | it x + y - ;
10 3 4 all-receive . cr  \ 3 (it = y = 4)
clear

\ === one frame carries user locals and both groups ===
: framed | >x | x 2 * this + ;
6 framed . cr          \ 24
' framed see-compiled
clear

\ === quotations read the definition's fixed values ===
: quoted [ 1 2 3 ] [: drop it :] map ;
9 quoted . cr          \ [ 9 9 9 ]
: mapped 2 * this drop [ 1 2 3 ] [: drop this :] map ;
7 mapped . cr          \ [ 14 14 14 ]
clear

\ === early exit unwinds the frame ===
: early it 5 > if "big" exit then it 2 * ;
9 early . cr           \ big
3 early . cr           \ 6
clear

\ === recursion fixes per activation ===
: countdown it 0 > if it 1 - countdown then ;
3 countdown .s cr      \ 3 2 1 0
clear
: countdown-this it 1 > if it 1 - countdown-this then this ;
3 countdown-this .s cr   \ 3 2 1 1 1 1
clear

\ === inline splice fixes at the call site ===
: sq it it * ; inline
: user 1 + sq ;
4 user . cr            \ 25
clear

\ === see shows the typed source; see-compiled shows the frame ===
' square see
' square see-compiled

\ === a local named it shadows the anaphor ===
: shadowed | >it | it 100 + ;
1 shadowed . cr        \ 101
clear

\ === a top-level quotation fixes on the line that runs it ===
5 [: it :] execute . cr        \ 5
clear
5 [: this 2 * :] execute . cr  \ 10
clear

\ === errors: too few values, or a fixing mention that reaches nothing ===
it                     \ (nothing on the stack where it is mentioned)
them                   \ (nothing on the stack; them needs 2)
7
them                   \ (one value; them needs 2)
other                  \ (one value; other needs 2)
that                   \ (one value; that needs 2)
clear
: strict it ;
strict                 \ (empty stack at word entry)
: strict2 them + ;
9 strict2              \ (one value at word entry; them needs 2)
: fix-in-branch dup 1 > if this 2 * nip then ;   \ (not the top level of the body)
: fix-in-quotation [: this :] execute ;          \ (same)
